    "insert": "The study explores how large language models (LLMs) like ChatGPT can assist primary school students \nin constructively learning from their mistakes, particularly in mathematics. \nIt focuses on using LLMs to create individualized learning paths, providing explanations \nand tailored tasks to address knowledge gaps. The research questions examine how ChatGPT \ncan effectively assist with maths tasks, the benefits and limitations for students and teachers, \nand how prompts can facilitate learning.\nTheoretical frameworks such as constructivism, the TPACK model, and self-determination theory \nhighlight how LLMs can enhance autonomy and engagement. However, \nintegrating LLMs into primary education remains underexplored, and existing studies primarily focus on older students. \nThe methodology involves instructing three primary school teachers in Darmstadt on ChatGPT use, with students using the tool \nto review and learn from maths errors. Surveys and teacher interviews will evaluate the tool’s effectiveness. \nThe study aims to reveal how LLMs can foster active learning through error analysis, though comparisons with non-AI methods are beyond its scope.\n\nThe language throughout the whole expose is difficult to read and understand, because of \nnumerous grammatical, lexical and spelling errors. To list them all would go beyond \nthe reasonable scope of this review. To prevent something like this from happening, \nspell and grammar checkers can be utilised. There is also the possibility of having \nsomeone else proofread the text to be able to revise it accordingly.\nThe motivation is extensively laid out, albeit anecdotal at times. This may be \ncounteracted by appealing directly to the reader, instead of simply stating what \nis believed to be a fact; catching their interest, by asking a thought-provoking \nquestion (in reference to the first sentence from the motivation \nsection). \nIt is not clear whether the research questions use the terms \"LLM\" and \"ChatGPT\"  \nInterchangeably. It is also not clear who the target group of the study is supposed to be.\nOnly teachers and students? Or also parents? Especially the parents, either need to \nbe specifically included (which would broaden the research questions further), or \nspecifically excluded from the target group, because these demographics may require \nentirely different approaches to teaching them how to effectively use an LLM.\nThis leads to confusion about the scope of the proposed research in general. \nThe fact that LLMs notoriously struggle with mathematics \nis also completely overlooked, and it may be advisable to incorporate knowledge about \nissues like this into the study. If the specific LLM that is used for the study is not \ncapable of outputting basic maths calculations correctly, the focus of the study \ncompletely shifts from evaluating a tool for a specific purpose, in a specific \nenvironment, to simply evaluating the tool for its capabilities. \n\nThe severe language deficits greatly hinder a qualitative review of the proposed \nresearch venture, and it is highly advisable to seek proficiency in that domain, \nbefore taking any further action in the direction of writing research papers on computer science topics. \nThis may seem harsh, but being able to communicate \neffectively is crucial to a fruitful scientific discussion, and English is the \nde facto standard in computer science. Options to achieve this contain, not exclusively, \nuniversity classes, online courses/apps, or books and other literary materials.\n"
